{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED AT RUNTIME — GUARD 3 fires: ACLI returns no top-level `id`\n\nAC 1 satisfied. The failing dedup layer is the PRIMARY (ID-identity) skip, and the specific\nguard is the **third** one in `_record_comment_id`\n(`dispatch_apply_phases.py:328-330`):\n\n```python\ncomment_id = add_comment_result.get(\"id\") if isinstance(add_comment_result, dict) else None\nif not comment_id:\n    return          # <-- silent no-op on EVERY successful Cloud post\n```\n\n`add_comment` on the Cloud/ACLI transport does **not** return a comment resource. It returns\nACLI's **batch mutation envelope**, which has no top-level `id` at all.\n\n### Runtime evidence\n\n**(a) The real ACLI envelope, observed live** (`acli 1.3.19-stable`, authenticated against\n`navasage.atlassian.net`). Non-mutating probe against a key that cannot exist, so nothing was\ncreated in Jira:\n\n```\n$ acli jira workitem comment create --key ZZNOPE-424242 --body \"...\" --json\n{\n  \"results\": [\n    { \"status\": \"FAILURE\",\n      \"message\": \"Issue does not exist or you do not have permission to see it.\",\n      \"id\": \"ZZNOPE-424242\" }\n  ],\n  \"totalCount\": 1,\n  \"successCount\": 1\n}\n```\n\nNote `results[].id` is the **work item key**, not a comment id — ACLI's comment-create is\nbatch-shaped (`--key` takes a LIST, plus `--jql`/`--filter`), so it reports per-work-item\nresults and never echoes the created comment resource. This is the same envelope the repo\nalready documents for the `comment` verb in `AcliMutationError`\n(`adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py:258-276`, bug 44de).\n\n**(b) The guard firing, observed executing.** `_record_comment_id` was instrumented\n(`DEBUG-rebar-debug`, since reverted) and driven through the REAL production code — real\n`acli_cli_ops.add_comment`, real `subprocess` via `_run_acli`, real `BindingStore`. The only\nsubstitution was the `acli` binary itself, emitting the envelope shape verified in (a):\n\n```\nDEBUG-rebar-debug GUARD-3 no-id result={'results': [{'status': 'SUCCESS', 'message': 'Comment added',\n                                         'id': 'REB-1861'}], 'totalCount': 1, 'successCount': 1}\nadd_comment returned top-level .get('id') -> None\ncomment_ids -> {}\nis_comment_mapped(key) -> False\n\nCONTROL (the shape the tests assume, {\"id\": \"10001\"}):\nDEBUG-rebar-debug RECORDED key=1787251802516868001 id=10001\ncomment_ids -> {\"1787251802516868001\": \"10001\"}\n```\n\nThe control proves guards 1, 2 and 4 and the persistence path are all healthy — the same call\nwith an id-bearing result records and saves.\n\n### The other four candidates, each ruled out\n\n| # | Candidate guard | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `binding_store is None` | RULED OUT | Threaded end-to-end: `reconcile.py:281` load -> `reconcile.py:526-548` -> `applier.py:488-495` -> `applier.py:641-646` ctx -> `apply_handlers.py:347-355` / `:140-148` -> `dispatch_one.py:475-477` / `:383`. No hop relies on the `None` default. Control run above records successfully. |\n| 2 | no `local_comment_key` | RULED OUT **at runtime** | Real production comment record for this very ticket (keys `author/author_email/author_id/body/timestamp`, `timestamp=1787251802516868001`) driven through the REAL `_diff_comments` emits 1 mutation carrying `local_comment_key -> 1787251802516868001`. |\n| 3 | `add_comment` result has no `id` | **CONFIRMED** | (a) + (b) above. |\n| 4 | store lacks `record_comment_id` | RULED OUT | `BindingStore.record_comment_id` exists (`binding_store.py:359`); control run recorded through it. `_EmptyBindings` is only used on the read-only `reconcile_check` path. |\n| 5 | written but never persisted | RULED OUT | `record_comment_id` saves IMMEDIATELY (`binding_lifecycle.py:496-497`); `BindingRepository.save()` writes the whole `_data` atomically (`binding_repository.py:262-279`), `comment_ids` is a top-level template key (`binding_repository.py:56-61`), and the workflow commits `.bridge_state/bindings.json` back (`reconcile.py:665-673`, `_store/push.py:428`). 2475 `bindings` entries prove the write path works. |\n\nGuards 1/2/4/5 are all empirically healthy, yet production `comment_ids` holds **0** entries\nafter thousands of successful posts. By elimination that is only possible if guard 3 fires on\nthe success path — i.e. the success payload also lacks a top-level `id`, exactly as the batch\nenvelope in (a) implies.\n\n### Why the symptom looked body-shaped\n\nWith the PRIMARY skip inert, the SECONDARY body-equality skip is the only one left, and it is\nlossy by construction (local fitted+normalized body vs. a body that round-tripped through\nADF/wiki). That is why the re-emission rate tracks comment richness (REB-1861 1/40 matched,\nREB-424 22/25) rather than any positional pattern. One cause explains every observed symptom.\n\n### Transport asymmetry — why every test passed\n\nThe DC/REST transport genuinely does return `{\"id\": ...}`\n(`adapters/jira_datacenter/_links.py:82-84` -> `_unwrap` -> `Comment.raw` from\n`POST /rest/api/2/issue/{key}/comment`). Only the **Cloud/ACLI CLI** path is broken. Every\ntest double asserts the id-bearing shape by assumption — `test_comment_id_capture.py:52,67,81`\n(`MagicMock`), and `test_acli_comment_adf_encode.py:32-33`, the one test that stubs actual acli\nstdout, hand-invents `'{\"id\": \"10001\"}'`. No recorded real comment-create payload exists\nanywhere in the repo. Note also that `add_comment` (`acli_cli_ops.py:528-529`) is the ONLY\n`--json` consumer in that module that skips the normalizing idiom every sibling uses\n(`_parse_acli_comments`, `_parse_paginated_comments`, `get_issue`, `_verify_created_issue`).\n\n### Defect attribution — `caused_by` 789c-1251-fc21-4cf6 (emersed-specific-mutt)\n\n`git blame -w -C -M -L 323,332 dispatch_apply_phases.py` -> **27b868ba55** (\"Append-only comment\nsync with comment-ID map\", `rebar-ticket: 789c-1251-fc21-4cf6`), which introduced\n`_record_comment_id` and every one of its guards. That commit is the origin, not merely a\ncarrier: it added a NEW consumer written against a producer contract it never verified. Ticket\n789c's own plan states \"an unmapped key is created in Jira and **its returned ID recorded**\" —\nthe Cloud transport never returned one. `acli_cli_ops.add_comment` predates the change and was\ncorrect-at-the-time (nothing read its return value until 789c did), so this is not the\nfix-site/cause-site trap: the obligation and the failing consumer were created by the same\ncommit.\n\nDivergence note: a whole-file blame majority would name the same commit here, so the considered\nattribution and the mechanical one agree.\n\nClose class: **`plan_defect`** — the plan's central assumption about the transport contract was\nwrong, so the feature shipped inert rather than regressing something that worked. Before 789c\nthere was no ID dedup at all; 789c promised it and delivered a silent no-op on Cloud.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect`\n\nCited authority: ticket 789c's acceptance criteria; the in-code contract on\n`BindingLifecycle.record_comment_id` (\"the durable entry is what the outbound differ's PRIMARY\nskip keys on, so a crash after the Jira post cannot re-post\"); and existing tests\n(`test_comment_id_capture.py`) that assert the map IS populated after a post. Observed\nbehaviour contradicts all three.\n\n### Caller-dependency sweep\n\nPost-fix behaviour: `comment_ids` gains an entry per successfully-posted comment, so\n`is_comment_mapped` returns True and the PRIMARY skip engages. The map's only PRODUCTION\nconsumer is `is_comment_mapped` at `outbound_comments.py:414`; `comment_id_for` has **no**\nproduction caller at all (tests only). Nothing depends on the map staying empty. Gate clear.\n\n### Not in scope here\n\nBug 1f3d (`florid-vigorous-urial`) fixed the *pagination* route to the same 5000-comment\ninflation; that fix is present and correct (`acli_cli_ops.py:583-600` `--paginate`, truncation\nguard at `outbound_comments.py:322-346`) and is untouched. Cleanup of already-posted duplicates\nremains a separate workstream (precedent: `bd80-0603-7d44-4d9f`)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787253493429763001,"uuid":"56ebb762-a8ff-44a1-b4ae-9f8a60a5ace9"}